'''
Machine Learning
-----------------
1. Types of Machine Learning
    a. Supervised Learning
        -> When labeled data is used.
        -> If our data has both features and target.

        Types of Supervised Learning
        i. Regression
            -> If we have to predict or recommend continuous data.

            Algorithms
            ------------
            a. Linear Regression
            b. Decision Tree Regressor and Random Forest Regressor
            c. KNN (K Nearest Neighbor) -> KNR (K Neighbor Regressor)
            d. SVM (Support Vector Machine) -> SVR (Support Vector Regressor)
            e. XGBoost Regressor

        ii. Classification
            -> If we have to predict or recommend discrete / categorical data.

            Algorithms
            ------------
            a. Logistic Regression
            b. Decision Tree Classifier and Random Forest Classifier
            c. KNN (K Nearest Neighbor) -> KNC (K Neighbor Classifier)
            d. SVM (Support Vector Machine) -> SVC (Support Vector Classifier)
            e. XGBoost Classifier
        
        
    b. Unsupervised Learning
        -> When unlabelled data is used.
        -> If our data only contains features.

        Types of Unsupervised Learning
        i. Clustering

        Algorithm
        -------------
        a. KMeans Clustering
        b. PCA (Principle Component Analysis)
        c. DBSCAN
        d. Agglomerative Clustering
        e. Hierarchical Clustering

2. Process of Implementing Machine Learning
    a. Read Data, Clean Data, Feature Engineering, Data Analysis, Data Visualization
    
    b. Separate Features and Target
        -> All Features must be independent to each other
        -> But Target must be depend on all features.
        -> Features must be multi dimension array.
        -> Target must be one dimension array.
    
    c. Train Test Split
        -> train_test_split(
            X, Y, test_size=0.3, random_state=42
        )

        Train Test Rule (80/20 Rule)
        - 80% Training Data -> Both Feature and Target Values
        - 20% Testing Data

        70/30, 60/40, 50/50
        
    d. Data Scaling
        -> To stop biasness
        i. Feature Scaling
            a. Standard Scaler
                - If our data is not in range.
                - (Xi - mean) / Sd
                - Best Output Range (-1 to 1)
                - Considerable Output Range (-3 to 3)
                
            b. Min Max Scaler
                - If our data is in range.
                - (Xi - Min_Value) / (Max_Value - Min_Value)
                - Output Range (0 to 1)
            
        ii. Label Encoder
            -> Convert Categorical Data into Numeric Data
            a. Label Encoding
                -> Sort category into ascending order
                -> Then gives sorted category a number starting from 0 to nth value.
                -> Then forms a new column
                
            b. Manual Encoding
            
            c. One Hot Encoding
                -> Sort category into ascending order
                -> Then gives sorted category a number starting from 0 to nth value.
                -> Each cateogry has its own column

    e. Define Model
    f. Fit Training Data into Model
    g. Predict Testing Data
    h. Metrics
    i. Visualization

ML Package
------------
Scikit-Learn (sklearn)
'''





'''
- Straight Line Concept
- y = mX + C
- y -> Predicted Value
- m -> Slope (B1)
- X -> Data Points
- C -> Intercept / Coefficient (Constant) (B0)

- Euclidean Distance

Metrics
----------
1. MAE (Mean Absolute Error)
    -> Distance between each data points and best fit line
    -> sum of all distance
    -> (x2 - x1) + (y2 - y1)

2. MSE (Mean Squared Error)
    -> Squared Distance between each data points and best fit line
    -> Sum of all squared distance
    -> (x2 - x1)2 + (y2 - y1)2

3. RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) -> Loss Function
    -> Root(MSE)
    -> Root((x2 - x1)2 + (y2 - y1)2)
    
4. R2 Score (Resudial Score -> E) -> Cost Function -> Accuracy

Rule
----
- If RMSE Increases R2 Score Decreases
- If RMSE Decreases R2 Score Increases -> Correct

Linear Regression Formula
---------------------------
-> If we have multiple features
mlr = B0 + B1.X1 + B2.X2 + ... + Bn.Xn + E

-> If we have single feature
slr -> B0 + B1.X1 + E

Suppose 4 Features -> different 4 Slopes (B1) and Single Intercept (B0)

Types of Linear Regression
---------------------------
1. Polynomial Regression
2. Ridge Regression
3. Lasso Regression
4. Elastic Net Regression
5. Cross Validation
'''





'''
-> Used for Binary Classification
    -> 0/1, True/False, Yes/No

-> Logistic Regression Runs on Sigmoid Function
    
1. Feature and Target
2. Train Test
    -> Stratification / Stratify (Do Stratify only for Target)
        - Target Data -> Must be Split Equally
        - 0 -> 60%
        - 1 -> 40%

        - Test-size -> 0.2
        -> 0 -> 10%, 1 - 10%

        train_test_split(
            X, Y, test_size=0.2, random_state=42, stratify=Y
        )
    
3. Feature Scaling
    -> Standard Scaler / Min Max Scaler

4. Define Model (Implementation of Model)
    LogisticRegression(
        solver= liblinear, lbfgs(default), saga
        class_weight = balanced, None (default)
        random_state = Same as train test split, None (default)
    )
    - Solver -> Choose according to data size
        -> liblinear -> Small  dataset (0 - 15/20k)
        -> lbfgs -> Small or Medium Dataset but specific use for medium dataset (>20k to <100k)
        -> Saga -> Large Dataset (>100k)

    - Class Weight -> compulsary for binary classification / optional for multi class classification
        -> When data fitted to model, make sure to distribute data equally of each class.
        -> Use class_weight = 'balanced'

    - Random State -> To split data from specific index

5. Fit Data
6. Predict Data
7. Metrics
    a. Classification Report
        - Precision -> Acccording to output accuracy of model, how much correct percentage class data will be predicted.
        - Recall -> According to support (number of dadta), how much correct precentage class data is predicted.
        - F1-Score -> Average of Precision and Recall
        - Support -> Number of Data of each classes.
        
    b. Confusion Matrix
        - X-axis -> Predicted Data
        - Y-axis -> Actual Data

        i. True Positive (TP) -> Patient in Data is healthy, Model also Predicts Healthy
        ii. False Positive (FP) -> Patient in Data is Healthy, But Model Predicts Unhealthy
        iii. False Negative (FN) -> Patient in Data is Unhealthy, But Model Predicts Healthy
        iv. True Negative (TN) -> Patient in Data is Unhealthy, But Model also Predicts Unhealthy
8. Visualization

9. Cross Validation
    -> Stratified K Fold
    
10. ROC/AUC Curve
    -> Create only for Binary Classification
    -> Threshold Choose
'''





'''
Lazy Algorithm

- While creating Elbow method we can create two conceptual figures.
    - Using Error Rate
    - Using Accuracy Score

- Problems of KNN Model
    - Model Overfit -> When model learns very low number of data
    - Model Underfit -> When model learns all data (correct value, outliers, null values)

- To solve overfit or underfit
    - We have to use cross validation concept.
        - Stratified K Fold
        - Grid Search Cross Validation

- Search Cross Validation
    - Grid Search Cross Validation
        - When model does less calculation
        
    - Random Search Cross Validation
        - When model does large calculation

- How does Elbow Method Forms?
    Total Students -> 20

    X-Train -> 16 Random Features, X-test -> 4 Random Features 
    Y-Train - 16 Random Targets, Y-test -> 4 Random Targets

    k_range = range(1, 21) # 1 - 20
    error_rate = []
    acc_score = []

    for i in k_range:
        model = KNeighborClassifier(n_neighbors = i) # 3
        model.fit(X_train, Y_train)
        pred = model.predict(X_test) # 
        error_rate.append(np.mean(Y_test != pred))
        acc_score.append(accuracy_score(Y_test, pred))

    Error Rate -> Total Wrong Prediction / Total Number of Test Data

    Test    Predict
    A         A -> Correct
    A         A -> Correct
    B         A -> Incorrect
    C         A -> Incorrect

    A         B
    A         B
    B         B -> Correct
    C         B

    K = 1 -> Error Rate -> 2/4 -> 0.5

    K = 2 -> Error Rate -> (A) -> 2/4 -> 0.5
                        -> (B) -> 1/4 -> 0.25 (Lowest)

    K = 3 -> Error Rate -> (B) -> 1/4 -> 0.25

- Process of KNN
    a. Data Wrangling, Visualization
    b. Feature and Target Selection
    c. Train Test Split
    d. Feature Scaling
    e. Elbow Method to find Optimal Number of Neighbor
    f. Model Implementation
    g. Metrics
    h. Visualization
    i. If needed -> Cross validation -> Stratified K Fold and Grid Search Cross Validation
'''





